/// Money a customer owes the farm for a confirmed `Sale` (RAKHAL-340, Finance
/// V2 — `FINANCE_V2_DESIGN.md` §4.3, §4.6). Created by the sale-type use
/// cases (RAKHAL-342/345/346/347) when a sale isn't fully paid at the moment
/// it's recorded — `ReceivableService.createReceivable` is the seam they call
/// as a later step of their own `$transaction`, after `Sale`+`SaleLine`
/// (RAKHAL-339).
///
/// **Outstanding and status are computed, never stored** — this is the
/// ticket's own core decision (`FINANCE_V2_DESIGN.md` §4.6, "Payment status
/// is derived, never chosen"). The V1 `FinanceReceivable`
/// (`prisma/schemas/finance/finance.prisma`) carried a `status`
/// `FULL`/`PARTIAL` column the farmer had to pick by hand; V2 deletes that
/// input entirely. `ReceivableService.getOutstanding`/`getStatus` derive both
/// at read time from `PaymentAllocation` rows summed against this
/// receivable's id (`SUM(PaymentAllocation.amount) WHERE receivableId = this`)
/// — no column here duplicates that state, so it can never drift out of sync
/// with the ledger the way V1's hand-set status could.
///
/// **`customerId` — same judgment call as `Sale.customerId`**
/// (`prisma/schemas/sales/sale.prisma`): no generic `Customer` entity exists
/// yet, so this stays a plain nullable scalar, not a `@relation` — mirrors
/// the sale it was created from (usually the identical value, but kept as
/// its own column so a receivable's counterparty can be looked up without a
/// join, and so a future ticket that adds a real `Customer` model can
/// migrate this column additively without touching `Sale`).
///
/// Closes the `TODO(RAKHAL-340)` `PaymentAllocation.receivableId` left as a
/// plain scalar by RAKHAL-337 (`payment.prisma`) — that column is now a real
/// `@relation` to this model (see `payment.prisma`'s own updated doc
/// comment).
model Receivable {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  farmId String

  /// The `Sale` this receivable was raised for. Real FK — unlike
  /// `PaymentAllocation.receivableId`/`payableId`'s placeholder-era scalar
  /// columns, `Sale` already exists (RAKHAL-339) by the time this model is
  /// created.
  saleId String @unique

  /// See this model's own doc comment above for the judgment call on why
  /// this is a plain nullable scalar, not a `@relation`.
  customerId String?

  /// Mirrors the `Sale.totalAmount` at the moment this receivable was
  /// raised — a sale is never edited when money arrives later
  /// (`FINANCE_V2_DESIGN.md` §4.5's worked example), so this column is a
  /// snapshot, not a live read of `Sale`.
  totalAmount Decimal @db.Decimal(18, 2)

  dueDate DateTime?

  /// Set once `getOutstanding` reaches zero — an operational bookkeeping
  /// timestamp only, **not** the source of truth for `PAID` (that's always
  /// derived from allocations; `closedAt` just records *when* it happened
  /// for reporting, and is written by `ReceivableService` alongside the
  /// allocation that closes it, not read back to decide status).
  closedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  farm Farm @relation("FarmToReceivable", fields: [farmId], references: [id])
  sale Sale @relation("SaleToReceivable", fields: [saleId], references: [id])

  allocations PaymentAllocation[] @relation("ReceivableToPaymentAllocation")

  @@index([farmId])
  @@index([farmId, customerId])
  @@index([farmId, dueDate])
}
